// The scene's light sources. See include/custom/SceneLights.hpp for the three
// types and what every field means.
//
//   direct   direction + color. No position, no decay.
//   point    position + color + decay (g, beta).
//   spot     a point light plus an aim direction and two cone angles.
//
//   g        distance at which the light is exactly its stated color
//   beta     falloff exponent: 0 constant, 1 inverse-linear, 2 inverse-square
//
// Positions are world coordinates, and "instance" + "offset" hangs a light off a
// scene.json instance instead of repeating its coordinates.
{
	// Indirect lighting, hemispheric (E07 slides 47-54): two colors blended by
	// which way a surface faces. "upper" is what a surface pointing at the sky
	// receives, "lower" what one pointing at the ground receives, "direction" is
	// the axis they blend along, i.e. world up.
	//
	// WARM, because the torches are the only thing burning in here.
	//
	// These used to be sky-blue above and courtyard-brown below, which was
	// correct while the scene had a sun: the hemisphere is a directional light's
	// indirect half, which is exactly why CookTorrance.frag excludes direct
	// lights from the per-light "bounce" term -- feeding the sun in there would
	// have doubled what these two colors already said. The sun is gone (see the
	// empty "lights" array below) and the dungeon is sealed: scene.json has
	// floor, ceiling, straight walls and corners, and its only "Hole" is
	// SM_WallDoor_Hole_01, the doorway. No window, no grate, no sky. So there is
	// no source in this level that could put blue light on anything, and the
	// blue survived here purely because nobody re-derived it after the sun left.
	//
	// What there IS is TORCH_LIGHT_COLOR (1.0, 0.5, 0.16, main.cpp) bouncing off
	// grey-brown stone. A bounce keeps the hue, loses a little saturation and a
	// lot of energy, which is what these two now describe.
	//
	// The separation between a lit room and a dead corridor therefore has to
	// come from LUMINANCE, not from hue -- everything down here is the same
	// color of fire, and only the amount of it changes. That is the honest
	// version of the warm/cool contrast the blue was faking, and it is why
	// these are darker than the values they replace (midpoint luminance ~0.38
	// against ~0.46) rather than just re-tinted: a warm ambient at the old
	// brightness reads as a second light source instead of as spill.
	//
	// Which is which: hemisphereColor() blends with w = (dot(N, direction) + 1)
	// / 2 and returns mix(lower, upper, w), so "upper" is what a surface facing
	// UP receives -- the floor, the carpet, the tabletop, the top of a barrel --
	// and "lower" what a surface facing DOWN receives, the ceiling and the
	// underside of things. Named for the direction the surface looks at, not for
	// where the surface sits, which is easy to get backwards.
	//
	// upper is the brighter of the two because an upward-facing surface in this
	// dungeon is looking at the flames: the torches are bracketed high on the
	// walls and burn upward, so the top half of the room is where the light is.
	// lower is darker because a downward-facing surface is looking at the floor,
	// which is the far end of every bounce in here.
	//
	// The split only reaches the models that actually blend. Material::
	// interiorAmbient pins the enclosed ones at w = 0.5 and hands them the
	// midpoint instead -- today that is 6 models (dungeonCeiling, the bookshelf
	// and book, and the three metals), against 13 that take the real blend,
	// dungeonFloor and dungeonWall among them. So these two values are not a
	// detail: getting them the wrong way round darkens every floor in the level.
	//
	// Read the pair as this renderer's stand-in ENVIRONMENT rather than as a
	// light with a name: besides being the diffuse indirect term, they are what
	// metalAmbient() reflects, the cheapest form of a split-sum IBL with two
	// colors where a real one has a prefiltered cube map. Which is also why
	// blacking them was the worst thing to try: it told every metal in the
	// scene it was standing in a void, and it made the ambient cheat INVERTED on
	// them -- switching it on cost them their share of the frame and returned
	// nothing. Warm is also the right answer for them: a steel chain in this
	// room reflects firelight, not daylight.
	//
	// These are COLORS ONLY, not the radiance itself, so they carry hue and
	// relative brightness while "weight" below sets how much of the frame they
	// get. They no longer run near 1 the way the sky ones did: with the whole
	// room one color of fire, how DARK the indirect term is at the bottom of
	// its range is the thing doing the work, so some of that is authored here
	// rather than all of it in a single scene-wide weight.
	// "weight" carries the brightness, because CookTorrance.frag blends the
	// ambient against the direct light (E17's LambertBlinnTexture.frag) instead
	// of adding it on top: direct keeps (1 - weight), ambient takes weight.
	// Adding it was what made the ambient a floor under every pixel -- see the
	// shader's comment at the blend for why a ceiling could not stop it.
	//
	// "weight" is the scene DEFAULT, and it is the INDOOR value, because the
	// game is played indoors: the player is inside the dungeon from the start
	// and only leaves it by winning. So the common case is an enclosed room,
	// the default matches it, and the handful of models under the open sky
	// carry an override in materials.json instead. The other way round -- which
	// this file briefly had -- leaves anything not listed there, i.e. the small
	// dungeon props, silently lit as if the ceiling weren't there.
	//
	// 0.05 is E17's value for its enclosed maze, and it is the one of the two
	// that gets looked at: the game is played indoors from spawn to win. The
	// outdoor override in materials.json (0.20) is untouched and untuned since
	// the sun was removed -- the exterior now has no direct light at all, so if
	// anyone ever does go and look at it, that number and the missing sun are
	// the same conversation, not this one.
	//
	// "bounce" is the OTHER half of the indirect term. Both halves are now the
	// same firelight off the same stone; what separates them is whether they
	// know WHERE the fire is. The hemisphere above is the part that does not --
	// a floor under the whole level, the light that has bounced enough times to
	// have forgotten its source. This is the part that does: every point and
	// spot light feeds the indirect bucket at this fraction of its radiance,
	// with its own decay, colour and flicker, so the room with a torch in it
	// gets more indirect light than the dead end down the corridor, and gets it
	// from the right direction.
	//
	// Wrapped rather than clamped -- (dot + 1) / 2, no terminator -- because
	// bounced light arrives from most of the hemisphere rather than from the
	// flame itself. Shadowed, though, like the direct term and by the same
	// visibility: a fragment that cannot see the flame should not collect a
	// fill sized by its distance to it, which is what put a glow at the near
	// end of every shadow before.
	//
	// 0.35 is an authored stand-in for "how much of what hits a stone wall
	// comes back", in the same spirit as upper/lower being authored colours.
	// It is a share of "weight", not an addition to it: the two sources are
	// summed inside the ambient bucket, which still cannot exceed weight of
	// the frame, so turning the Ambient Light cheat off removes both.
	//
	// What it fixes: with the hemisphere alone, Material::interiorAmbient pins
	// the blend at 0.5 and every dungeon surface gets the same flat midpoint,
	// identical in a lit room and in a dead-end corridor, because a constant
	// cannot tell them apart. Now the corridor with no torch collects the
	// midpoint and nothing else, while the lit room gets that plus the bounce
	// of the torch actually in it.
	//
	// With both halves warm, this is also the whole of the scene's contrast: the
	// two rooms differ in how MUCH firelight they hold, not in its colour. Which
	// is what a dungeon lit only by torches actually looks like, and is why the
	// midpoint here (0.60, 0.34, 0.15) is deliberately dark -- it is the bottom
	// of the range, the value a room reaches when no flame reaches it at all.
	"ambient": {
		"upper": [0.75, 0.42, 0.18],
		"lower": [0.45, 0.26, 0.12],
		"direction": [0.0, 1.0, 0.0],
		"weight": 0.05,
		"bounce": 0.35
	},

	"lights": [
		// The sun is GONE. It used to sit here as the scene's one "direct"
		// light, parked ~55 degrees above the horizon, tinted warm, casting
		// through the single 2D shadow map:
		//
		//   { "id": "sun", "type": "direct",
		//     "direction": [0.25, -0.82, -0.52], "color": [1.0, 0.98, 0.92],
		//     "orbitSpeed": 0.0, "castsShadow": true }
		//
		// Kept here as a comment because putting it back is one paste: nothing
		// in the engine was removed with it. SceneLights still loads "direct"
		// lights, main.cpp still builds the sun's orthographic light-space
		// matrix for any light that asks for a shadow (SUN_TARGET /
		// SUN_ORTHO_HALF_EXTENT there), and the 2D shadow slots are still
		// allocated -- they just go unused now, since no light in this file
		// claims one.
		//
		// Its indirect half went with it: see "upper"/"lower" above, both black.
		//
		// The dungeon wall torches (torchW1/W2, torchE1/E2, torchDC, and the
		// dv alcove's torchDV) used to be authored here too, hung off their
		// instances with "instance" + "offset". They were removed: every one of
		// them also carries the "dungeonTorch" model, so flames.json spawns a
		// TorchFlame for each of them independently (main.cpp's "Torch
		// flames' point lights" block), which appends its own per-frame point
		// light into gubo already -- correct position even while the flame
		// billboard sways, flicker, and a shot at the dynamic shadow-cube
		// pool. Keeping a second, static copy here just doubled each torch's
		// light contribution and doubled its debug gizmo (two crosses per
		// torch, and since only the TorchFlame copy is distance-culled, only
		// one of the pair would disappear when walking away). TorchFlame is
		// now the sole owner of these six; nothing else in this file
		// duplicates a flame-driven instance.
	]
}
